21:33:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Doctors do say that, but usually “don’t strain your heart” means something more specific than “every heartbeat uses up a limited supply.” After a heart scare, the heart or its blood supply may be temporarily—or permanently—less able to handle sudden high demand. For example: - If coronary arteries are narrowed, intense exertion can make the heart need more oxygen than it can receive, causing angina or, in some cases, a heart attack. - After a heart attack, procedure, myocarditis, rhythm problem, or heart-failure episode, the heart may need time to heal and medications may need adjustment. - Heavy lifting or all-out exertion can sharply raise blood pressure and put a sudden load on the heart. - An arrhythmia can be provoked or worsened by intense exertion in some people. So “take it easy” often means: **avoid abrupt or maximal exertion until we know what is going on and until you can safely rebuild capacity.** It does not usually mean that gentle-to-moderate activity is inherently damaging. In fact, after many cardiac events, doctors commonly prescribe or recommend **cardiac rehabilitation**: supervised, gradually progressive aerobic exercise, education, and risk-factor treatment. That is because the right amount of activity can improve heart function, exercise tolerance, blood pressure, circulation, and long-term outcomes—even in many people with established heart disease. The analogy is less “the engine is running out of cycles” and more “an injured or under-supplied muscle should not be pushed to its limit before it has been evaluated and rehabilitated.” Your uncle’s individual restrictions should come from his own clinician, because the safe exercise level depends on what his “heart scare” actually was.
