21:34:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not totally wrong—it is an imprecise everyday phrase that contains an important truth. When people say “don’t overwork your heart,” they usually mean something like: - Don’t repeatedly push to exhaustion with little recovery. - Don’t do sudden maximal exertion if you are untrained or have risk factors. - Don’t ignore chest pain, fainting, severe breathlessness, or palpitations. - Don’t force intense activity when you are sick, dehydrated, sleep-deprived, or recovering from a cardiac problem. - Control conditions that make the heart work too hard, such as high blood pressure, blocked arteries, valve disease, anemia, or abnormal rhythms. Those are sensible cautions. A heart can indeed be harmed by chronic excessive pressure load, inadequate oxygen supply, inflammation, certain drugs, and—in some people—very extreme endurance training. What is misleading is interpreting the phrase as: **“The heart has a finite number of contractions, and every extra beat from normal exercise permanently consumes it.”** That is not how human hearts work, and it does not fit the observed evidence that regular, appropriately dosed activity lowers cardiovascular disease and mortality risk. Many common sayings survive because they are useful rules of thumb, not because they are literally precise. “Don’t wear yourself out” is good advice if it means avoid chronic overload and respect recovery. But it should not be taken to mean “avoid getting your heart rate up.” A more accurate version would be: > Your heart benefits from regular, manageable challenge and recovery; it can be harmed by extreme, prolonged, poorly recovered strain—especially when disease is present. That is broadly similar to the rest of the body. Walking normally does not “use up” a healthy joint; it generally helps preserve function. But running through an injury, carrying excessive loads for years, or doing repetitive work without recovery can contribute to damage. The heart is not exempt from limits; it just is not a disposable engine with a fixed beat counter.
