21:35:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are not dumb. Your intuition is reasonable: bodies can be harmed by overwork, people do get heart problems after years of strain, and doctors do tell some people not to strain themselves. Those observations are real. The issue is just that the simple phrase “the heart wears out from use” bundles together several different things: - normal exercise that usually helps, - very heavy or prolonged exertion that can be risky, - disease that makes exertion unsafe, - aging and accumulated damage from blood pressure, artery disease, and other factors. It makes sense that the everyday explanation feels convincing, because it captures the caution part: don’t push endlessly, don’t ignore symptoms, and do not assume you can safely go from inactive to maximal effort. A more accurate version does not require throwing out your whole belief: > Hearts are living organs that can be harmed by chronic overload and disease, and they need recovery; but regular, manageable exercise generally helps maintain their capacity rather than using them up. That is not “you were wrong about everything.” It is refining a common rule of thumb so it is useful without accidentally discouraging the kind of activity that protects most people’s hearts.
