21:36:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is reasonable to remain skeptical. The heart does age, and years of illness, high blood pressure, poor blood flow, inflammation, smoking, sleep problems, and excessive physical stress can contribute to deterioration. It is not immune to cumulative damage. The specific point the evidence does not support is that **normal, appropriately dosed activity causes that deterioration simply because it makes the heart beat more.** In population studies, regularly active people generally have better heart function and lower rates of heart attack, heart failure, stroke, and early death than similarly situated inactive people. So you do not have to accept an “exercise can’t hurt you” message—because that would be false. A cautious position is: - Avoid abrupt, maximal, or chronic exhaustion-level exertion. - Build fitness gradually and recover adequately. - Take symptoms seriously. - Follow individualized advice if you have heart disease or major risk factors. - But do not avoid ordinary walking, cycling, swimming, etc. out of fear that those activities are consuming the heart. You can think of it as the heart deteriorating from **adverse workload and disease**, not from all workload. The safest practical approach for most people is moderate activity, not inactivity and not relentless extreme training.
