21:36:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It survived because it is **not flat-out wrong** as a practical warning. It reflects real observations people made long before modern cardiology: - People sometimes collapsed after heavy exertion. - People with chest pain, breathlessness, or weakness often did worse if they pushed through it. - Hard labor, heat, poor nutrition, infections, and little recovery could leave people exhausted or ill. - Before blood-pressure treatment, cholesterol treatment, cardiac rehabilitation, and emergency care, many people had unrecognized heart disease and exertion could expose it dangerously. From those observations, “don’t overtax your heart” is a sensible folk rule. The problem is only if it gets interpreted as a precise biological claim: “every extra heartbeat permanently uses up a nonrenewable supply.” Traditional wisdom often survives because it is useful at the level of behavior even if its explanation is incomplete. “Rest when you are sick,” “don’t work yourself to collapse,” and “don’t ignore pain or breathlessness” remain good advice. Modern evidence adds an important qualification: **avoiding all exertion is not protective either.** Gradual, regular activity helps most hearts withstand demands better. So your grandparents’ warning can be translated into modern terms: > Don’t impose more demand than your body—and especially your heart—can safely handle, and allow recovery. That is consistent with recommending moderate exercise. A brisk walk done regularly is usually not “overtaxing” a healthy heart; trying to do maximal physical labor or a hard race while untrained, ill, dehydrated, or carrying undiagnosed heart disease might be.
