21:37:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is true that the heart does not remain unchanged forever. Decades of nonstop pumping occur alongside normal biological aging: cells accumulate damage, repair processes become less effective, connective tissue can stiffen, and blood vessels often stiffen too. So in that broad sense, a heart can become less resilient over a lifetime. But that is still different from a machine-like claim that each beat mechanically consumes a fixed amount of the heart or that a person has a preset “beat budget.” The heart is continuously supplied with oxygen and nutrients, continually replaces many of its cellular components, and adjusts its structure and function over time. Its decline with age reflects a mix of aging biology, genetics, blood-vessel changes, accumulated exposures, and disease—not simply the count of contractions. A useful way to phrase it is: - **Every heart has finite durability.** It can age, remodel, and eventually fail under some circumstances. - **But beat count is not the main known determinant of that durability.** The environment in which it beats matters much more: blood pressure, coronary blood flow, metabolic health, smoking, inflammation, sleep, kidney function, infections, drugs, and so on. - **Normal physical activity generally improves that environment.** So yes, continuous operation over 70–80 years is part of why aging is biologically meaningful. But it does not follow that reducing healthy activity preserves the heart. In practice, inactivity usually worsens the conditions that make the heart age and fail.
