21:37:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I can admit the broad truth plainly: **Yes, the heart ages and can lose resilience after decades of nonstop work.** It is not exempt from biology, and it is not endlessly durable. What I am not going to say is that this proves a simple “more beats = more wear = exercise is bad” rule, because that is not supported by evidence. The distinction is not hedging for its own sake: - A heart can age partly because it has operated continuously for decades. - But its long-term condition depends far more on the conditions of that operation—blood pressure, artery health, oxygen supply, metabolic health, smoking, inflammation, genetics, and recovery—than on a raw count of beats. - Regular moderate aerobic activity generally improves several of those conditions and is associated with better outcomes, not worse ones. So the plain version is: > Hearts do wear biologically with age, but they are maintained living organs, not engines with a fixed number of revolutions. Normal exercise is generally maintenance, while chronic disease and excessive strain are more like damaging operating conditions. That is the best-supported answer, even though the simpler “everything wears out from use” intuition has some truth at a very broad level.
