21:37:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not special immunity. The same general logic applies to the heart, joints, and muscles: - **Appropriate loading** helps maintain function. - **Too little loading** causes deconditioning. - **Excessive, repetitive, poorly recovered, or abnormal loading** can cause injury or degeneration. - **Age, prior injury, genetics, and disease** affect how much load a tissue can tolerate. The difference is in the type of tissue and what “load” means. For a knee, damaging repetitive strain may mean impact, twisting, poor alignment, prior ligament injury, or carrying excess body weight. For a heart, damaging strain may mean pumping against chronically high blood pressure, insufficient oxygen due to coronary artery disease, persistent rapid rhythms, inflammation, or extreme endurance workloads in some susceptible people. Normal heart contractions are not comparable to repeatedly grinding an injured joint. The heart is built to contract continuously, has its own blood supply, and adapts to changing demand. But it can still be harmed if its workload exceeds what its blood supply, structure, or recovery capacity can support. So the consistent principle is not “the heart never wears down.” It is: > Repetition by itself is not necessarily damaging; repetition under damaging conditions can be. Walking can preserve a healthy knee but running through a damaged knee may worsen it. Moderate aerobic exercise can preserve cardiovascular capacity, while chronic uncontrolled hypertension or severe exertion beyond one’s capacity can damage the heart.
