21:38:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it feels that way. “Things wear out when used” is a powerful everyday rule, and it is often correct for tools and for some kinds of bodily overuse injuries. But saying the heart benefits from appropriately dosed activity is not a contrarian position. It is mainstream clinical guidance from cardiology and public-health organizations, based on long-term studies and on cardiac-rehabilitation practice. Cardiologists routinely prescribe graded exercise after many heart events because avoiding all exertion generally makes patients less fit and can worsen outcomes. The common-sense statement can be retained in a more accurate form: > **Don’t chronically overload an aging, injured, or diseased heart.** > **Do regularly challenge a healthy or medically cleared heart within its capacity.** Those statements are compatible. The disagreement is not about whether the heart can be harmed; it clearly can. It is about whether its normal contractions—or ordinary exercise simply because it adds beats—are the main mechanism of that harm. They are not.
