21:38:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because the phrase can be harmless **if people understand it as “avoid dangerous overexertion and respect symptoms.”** That is good advice. It becomes less harmless if it leads someone to avoid walking, cycling, cardiac rehabilitation, or other manageable activity out of fear of “using up” their heart. Inactivity is a major, common cardiovascular risk factor, so the wording can affect real choices. But you are right about priorities: the bigger, well-established risks for most people are things like smoking, uncontrolled blood pressure, high LDL cholesterol, diabetes, sedentary time, poor sleep, heavy alcohol or drug use, and ignoring warning symptoms. No one needs to obsess over heartbeats or try to minimize them. The practical message can stay very simple: - Don’t push yourself to collapse or through alarming symptoms. - Build exercise gradually and recover. - Get medical guidance after a heart event or if you have concerning symptoms or significant risk factors. - Stay regularly active at a tolerable level. - Address the major cardiovascular risks. That preserves the useful caution in “don’t overwork your heart” without turning it into fear of normal activity.
