21:38:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The heart **does** follow the same broad rule: it ages along with the rest of the body. Over time, heart muscle and blood vessels can become stiffer, the heart may relax less well, and reserve capacity can decline. It is not exempt. The correction is only to the word **“use.”** Aging changes in hair, skin, bone, joints, and heart are not determined by one simple count of how often each part was used: - Skin ages from intrinsic biology plus sun exposure, smoking, nutrition, and other factors. - Bones often weaken more with **too little** weight-bearing use; resistance and impact exercise can help preserve them. - Muscles shrink with inactivity but can retain or regain function with training even in old age. - Joints can be harmed by injury and overload, yet ordinary movement usually helps maintain function. - Hearts age, but regular appropriately dosed activity tends to preserve capacity and reduce the diseases that accelerate heart decline. So the heart is not different from the body; it follows the same more nuanced biological pattern: > Aging happens, but healthy, manageable use often maintains tissue; disease, injury, and chronic overload accelerate deterioration. That applies to bones and muscles as much as it does to the heart.
