21:39:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—if I had said the heart “never runs down,” that would be ridiculous. I’m not saying that. A heart is finite, ages, and can gradually lose reserve even without a dramatic illness. Like all living tissue, it experiences molecular damage, changes in connective tissue, changes in energy production, and reduced repair capacity over time. It does not last forever. The disagreement is about whether that fact implies a simple rule that every additional normal heartbeat is inherently harmful. It doesn’t. Living organs are maintained and remodeled while they work. Their aging is influenced by time and cumulative biology, but also strongly by the quality of the conditions they operate under. A fair summary is: - **The heart is not perpetual.** It ages and has limits. - **It is not a disposable engine with a beat counter.** More normal activity does not automatically mean faster decline. - **Too much strain, too little recovery, or disease can accelerate decline.** - **Regular moderate activity generally preserves cardiovascular function and lowers disease risk.** That is not claiming immunity from aging; it is distinguishing normal use from damaging overload.
