21:39:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Long-lived ideas often contain a real observation, without being literally true in every detail. “Don’t overwork your heart” likely persisted because people could see that exhausting labor, heat, illness, poor nutrition, and pushing through chest pain or breathlessness sometimes preceded collapse or decline. Before modern diagnosis and treatment, people had no way to distinguish coronary disease, hypertension, anemia, infection, arrhythmias, or simple fatigue. A broad caution against overexertion was sensible and protective. That does not establish the stronger claim that normal lifelong use—or a higher number of heartbeats—mechanically wears the heart out. Many durable beliefs work that way: they are memorable rules that reduce risk, even if the mechanism is simplified. A version that respects the traditional wisdom *and* the evidence is: > Do not chronically exceed your body’s capacity, especially when ill, untrained, injured, or symptomatic; build capacity gradually and allow recovery. That remains good advice. It also leaves room for the equally well-supported point that regular moderate movement is generally one of the things that helps people stay healthier as they age.
