21:39:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are right that I have been too focused on correcting the oversimplified version and not clearly acknowledging the basic intuition you are trying to express. That would be frustrating. So, plainly: **Yes: the heart is a physical, living organ that works continuously for decades, and it ages. Lifelong cumulative operation is part of its finite durability.** It is reasonable to say that a heart can gradually “wear down” in the broad biological sense, just as other organs and tissues do. Where I was drawing a distinction—perhaps too insistently—is that medicine cannot reduce that wear to a simple rule of “more beats or more ordinary exercise always means faster deterioration.” A heart’s aging reflects both time spent working and the conditions under which it works: blood pressure, blood supply, metabolism, inflammation, genetics, recovery, and so on. But your core point does not need to be dismissed for that reason: > A heart is not inexhaustible; decades of nonstop pumping and accumulated strain are part of why it can weaken with age. That is fair. The practical addition is only that avoiding normal activity is not known to preserve it, while chronic overexertion or disease can make the long-term burden worse.
